---
name: scljet-sql-double-equals
description: SclJet SQL — `==` is SQLite's equality alias and must behave exactly like `=` on the scanned AND the indexed path
backends: [int, js]
---

# scljet — `==` is the same operator as `=`

`BUGS.md` `scljet-sql-double-equals-parser-gap`. `SELECT id FROM t WHERE v == 2` returned
`QUERY-ERROR:expected an expression operand` where SQLite 3.51.0 returns the row. It was a LEXER
gap, not a parser one: `=` was emitted one character at a time, so `==` became two `=` tokens and
the parser saw `v = = 2`. `isCompareOp` accepted the string `"=="` and `compareValue` normalized
it — both unreachable, which is the "internally inconsistent accepted operator set" the report
names.

The alias is now normalized in the tokenizer, so it reaches every path at once. Two of those paths
are pinned here because they are **different code**: a scanned table, and an INDEXED one whose
`=` is turned into an index range by a mapper that only ever accepted the single-character
spelling. Each query is run twice — once with `=`, once with `==` — and the pair must agree; a
regression that broke one spelling would show as a mismatched pair, not as a missing line.

`<>` and `!=` are here as the guard that the operator set is otherwise unchanged, and `x = = 2`
(with a space) is here because only ADJACENT characters may merge — that one must stay an error.

The imports sit ABOVE the fence, as in the sibling scljet cases: an import written *inside* a fence
is only followed by the INT lane (`BUGS.md` `js-jvm-codegen-in-fence-imports-not-followed`), and
this case must fail for its own reason or not at all.

[ByteSlice, SqliteValue, SqlNull, SqlInteger, SqlReal, SqlText, buildTableDatabase, buildTableWithIndexDatabase](std/scljet/index.ssc)
[queryImageParams](std/scljet/sql.ssc)

```scalascript
def showValue(v: SqliteValue): String = v match
  case SqlNull => "NULL"
  case SqlInteger(x) => x.toString
  case SqlText(x) => x
  case _ => "?"

def showRow(row: List[SqliteValue]): String = row match
  case Nil => ""
  case h :: Nil => showValue(h)
  case h :: t => showValue(h) + "|" + showRow(t)

def showRows(rows: List[List[SqliteValue]]): String = rows match
  case Nil => "(no rows)"
  case h :: Nil => showRow(h)
  case h :: t => showRow(h) + " ; " + showRows(t)

def dump(image: ByteSlice, label: String, sql: String): Unit =
  queryImageParams(image, sql, Nil) match
    case Left(message) => println(label + "=QUERY-ERROR:" + message)
    case Right(rows) => println(label + "=" + showRows(rows))

def probes(image: ByteSlice, prefix: String): Unit =
  dump(image, prefix + "-single", "SELECT id FROM cmp WHERE n = 20 ORDER BY id")
  dump(image, prefix + "-double", "SELECT id FROM cmp WHERE n == 20 ORDER BY id")
  dump(image, prefix + "-text-single", "SELECT id FROM cmp WHERE t = 'b' ORDER BY id")
  dump(image, prefix + "-text-double", "SELECT id FROM cmp WHERE t == 'b' ORDER BY id")
  dump(image, prefix + "-ne-angle", "SELECT id FROM cmp WHERE n <> 20 ORDER BY id")
  dump(image, prefix + "-ne-bang", "SELECT id FROM cmp WHERE n != 20 ORDER BY id")
  dump(image, prefix + "-scalar-double", "SELECT id FROM cmp WHERE (n == 20) = 1 ORDER BY id")
  dump(image, prefix + "-spaced", "SELECT id FROM cmp WHERE n = = 20 ORDER BY id")

val rows = List(
  List(SqlInteger(1L), SqlInteger(10L), SqlText("a")),
  List(SqlInteger(2L), SqlInteger(20L), SqlText("b")),
  List(SqlInteger(3L), SqlInteger(20L), SqlText("c")),
  List(SqlInteger(4L), SqlInteger(30L), SqlText("b"))
)

buildTableDatabase(512, 1, 1, "cmp",
  "CREATE TABLE cmp(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, n, t TEXT)", rows) match
  case Left(error) => println("build-error:" + error.message)
  case Right(base) => probes(base, "scan")

buildTableWithIndexDatabase(512, 1, 1, "cmp",
  "CREATE TABLE cmp(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, n, t TEXT)",
  "idx_n", "CREATE INDEX idx_n ON cmp(n)", List(1), rows) match
  case Left(error) => println("indexed-build-error:" + error.message)
  case Right(indexed) => probes(indexed, "index")
```
